{"prompt": {"messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a helpful assistant that repairs Python 3 code so that it correctly solves the given programming problem. The code reads and writes I/O from the console."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Repair the following Python 3 code so that it correctly solves the programming problem. The code reads and writes I/O from the console. Your repaired code should implement the problem correctly, meaning that it should produce the correct output for any valid input.\nPrint your repaired code and also the confidence score you give to your repair. The confidence score should be a number between 0 and 1. The format of the output should be:\n<repaired_code>\n<your_repaired_code>\n</repaired_code>\n<confidence>\n<your_confidence_score>\n</confidence>\nDo not provide any other text or markdown fences.\n\nHere is the problem description:\n<problem_description>\nSometimes one has to spell email addresses over the phone. Then one usually pronounces a dot as dot, an at sign as at. As a result, we get something like vasyaatgmaildotcom. Your task is to transform it into a proper email address (vasya@gmail.com). It is known that a proper email address contains only such symbols as . @ and lower-case Latin letters, doesn't start with and doesn't end with a dot. Also, a proper email address doesn't start with and doesn't end with an at sign. Moreover, an email address contains exactly one such symbol as @, yet may contain any number (possible, zero) of dots. You have to carry out a series of replacements so that the length of the result was as short as possible and it was a proper email address. If the lengths are equal, you should print the lexicographically minimal result. Overall, two variants of replacement are possible: dot can be replaced by a dot, at can be replaced by an at. \n</problem_description>\n\nHere is the Python 3 code you have to repair:\n<original_code>\n<your_rewritten_code_3>\nimport re\n\ns = input()\n\n# Replace first occurrence of \"at\" with \"@\" and \"dot\" with \".\"\ns = re.sub(r'at', '@', s, count=1)\ns = re.sub(r'dot', '.', s, count=1)\n\n# Handle leading special characters\nif s and s[0] == '.':\n    s = 'dot' + s[1:]\nelif s and s[0] == '@':\n    s = 'at' + s[1:]\n\n# Handle trailing special characters\nif s and s[-1] == '.':\n    s = s[:-1] + 'dot'\nelif s and s[-1] == '@':\n    s = s[:-1] + 'at'\n\nprint(s)\n</your_rewritten_code_3>\n</original_code>\n"}], "temp": 0, "sample_size": 1, "logprobs": true, "model": "qwen/qwen3-coder-next"}, "response": {"samples": [{"content": "<repaired_code>\nimport re\n\ns = input()\n\n# We need to find all occurrences of \"at\" and \"dot\" and decide which to replace\n# Since we want the shortest possible result (and both replacements have same length),\n# and lexicographically minimal if tied, we should replace all \"at\" with \"@\" and all \"dot\" with \".\"\n\n# But we need to ensure the result is a valid email:\n# - Contains exactly one '@'\n# - Contains only '.', '@', and lowercase letters\n# - Doesn't start or end with '.' or '@'\n# - May have any number of dots\n\n# Strategy: Replace all \"at\" with \"@\" and all \"dot\" with \".\", then fix invalid cases\n\n# First, replace all occurrences\ns = s.replace('at', '@')\ns = s.replace('dot', '.')\n\n# Now we need to ensure exactly one '@'\n# Count '@' symbols\nat_count = s.count('@')\n\n# If no '@', we need to create one - but this shouldn't happen in valid input\n# If multiple '@', we need to convert some back to 'at'\n\n# Since the problem states it's a transformed email, there should be exactly one '@' in the original encoding\n# But \"at\" in the middle of words might have been replaced\n\n# Let's reconsider: the input contains only \"dot\" and \"at\" as special encodings\n# So we should replace all \"dot\" with \".\" and all \"at\" with \"@\", but be careful about boundaries\n\n# Actually, the problem says: \"dot can be replaced by a dot, at can be replaced by an at\"\n# This means we can choose which occurrences to replace\n\n# Since we want shortest result, we should replace all \"at\" with \"@\" and all \"dot\" with \".\"\n# But we need exactly one \"@\"\n\n# Let's replace all \"dot\" first, then handle \"at\"\ns = s.replace('dot', '.')\n\n# Now replace all but one \"at\" with \"at\" (i.e., keep only one \"@\")\nat_positions = []\nfor i in range(len(s)):\n    if s[i] == '@':\n        at_positions.append(i)\n\n# If we have more than one '@', convert extra ones back to 'at'\nif len(at_positions) > 1:\n    # Convert all '@' except the first one back to 'at'\n    s_list = list(s)\n    for pos in at_positions[1:]:\n        s_list[pos] = 'a'\n        # Insert 't' after\n        s_list.insert(pos+1, 't')\n    s = ''.join(s_list)\n\n# Now handle leading/trailing special characters\n# If starts with '.' or '@', we need to convert the first part\nif s and s[0] == '.':\n    # Replace leading '.' with 'dot'\n    s = 'dot' + s[1:]\nelif s and s[0] == '@':\n    s = 'at' + s[1:]\n\nif s and s[-1] == '.':\n    s = s[:-1] + 'dot'\nelif s and s[-1] == '@':\n    s = s[:-1] + 'at'\n\n# Also need to handle cases where there's no '@' at all\nif '@' not in s:\n    # Find first 'at' in original encoding and convert to '@'\n    # But since we already processed, let's find where we can insert '@'\n    # Actually, we should have exactly one '@' in a valid email\n    # If still no '@', we need to create one by converting some 'at' back\n    # This is complex, so let's try a different approach\n\n# Let's use a cleaner approach: replace all \"dot\" with \".\", then replace first \"at\" with \"@\", others stay as \"at\"\ns = input()\ns = s.replace('dot', '.')\n# Replace first occurrence of \"at\" with \"@\"\ns = s.replace('at', '@', 1)\n\n# Now handle cases where we need to convert back due to boundary issues\n# If starts with '@', convert first '@' back to 'at' and find next 'at' to convert\nif s and s[0] == '@':\n    # Find first '@' and convert to 'at', then find next 'at' to convert to '@'\n    first_at = s.find('@')\n    s = s[:first_at] + 'at' + s[first_at+1:]\n    # Find next 'at' (which might be part of original text or from conversion)\n    next_at = s.find('at')\n    if next_at != -1:\n        s = s[:next_at] + '@' + s[next_at+2:]\n\nif s and s[-1] == '@':\n    # Find last '@' and convert to 'at', then find previous 'at' to convert to '@'\n    last_at = s.rfind('@')\n    s = s[:last_at] + 'at' + s[last_at+1:]\n    # Find 'at' before this position\n    prev_at = s.rfind('at', 0, last_at)\n    if prev_at != -1:\n        s = s[:prev_at] + '@' + s[prev_at+2:]\n\n# Handle leading/trailing dots\nif s and s[0] == '.':\n    s = 'dot' + s[1:]\nif s and s[-1] == '.':\n    s = s[:-1] + 'dot'\n\nprint(s)\n</repaired_code>\n<confidence>\n0.3\n</confidence>", "token_logprobs": [-5.960462772236497e-07, -0.0008212531101889908, -1.311301275563892e-06, -3.576278118089249e-07, -0.3910330533981323, -0.0005277194431982934, -0.0015044810716062784, -0.00043215948971919715, -1.1920928244535389e-07, -1.5497195136049413e-06, -0.0009199915803037584, -4.768360213347478e-06, -0.5238240957260132, -0.00903045292943716, -4.124556289752945e-05, -0.3098827600479126, -0.18277022242546082, -0.015545384027063847, -9.775113539944869e-06, -0.0007159530650824308, -0.11286505311727524, -1.1920922133867862e-06, -9.179073458653875e-06, -3.6954811548639555e-06, -7.271740287251305e-06, -0.0008704449282959104, -0.6480050086975098, -0.15536676347255707, -0.22654713690280914, -0.3918108344078064, -0.0010162194957956672, -0.001739775761961937, -7.152555099310121e-07, -0.6917843818664551, -0.5821159482002258, -0.001973706530407071, -0.034773580729961395, -6.282132380874828e-05, -0.3538509011268616, -0.004161508288234472, -0.4565492272377014, -0.12064599990844727, -0.5203471779823303, -0.3896907567977905, -0.11529668420553207, -0.0019546705298125744, -0.0007266741595230997, -0.35584190487861633, -2.3841855067985307e-07, -0.2274785041809082, -0.3185216188430786,